Scenario
As a Lead Cloud Security Engineer at Cymbal Group, you are tasked with securing the organization's new Generative AI platform. The development team has built an ADK agent (hello-world-agent) that connects to a serverless Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool service (agents-everywhere-sample-tool) to perform lookups.

To satisfy strict enterprise compliance requirements, you must do the following:

Deploy a private, governed network perimeter using a custom virtual private cloud (VPC), subnets, and an Internal Application Load Balancer as an Agent Gateway.
Deploy and register both the tool microservice (Cloud Run) and the Agent Runtime agent (Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform) in the project's Agent Registry.
Restrict egress pathways so that the tool's execution is gated by Identity-Aware Proxy (IAP), requiring authenticated Workload Identity SPIFFE tokens.
Protect against malicious prompt injections and sensitive data leaks (e.g., Social Security Numbers) by enabling Model Armor and Sensitive Data Protection guardrails inline at the gateway edge.
Are you ready for the challenge?

Task 1. Prepare the environment and download the lab files
In this task, you open the Cloud Shell terminal, download the pre-staged lab architecture files from a Google Cloud Storage bucket, and configure your administrative environment variables and tooling.

In the Google Cloud console title bar, click Activate Cloud Shell (Activate Cloud Shell).

When prompted to authorize Cloud Shell, click Authorize.

Run the following commands to export your core Google Cloud project identifiers and configure billing overrides for sensitive data inspection:

export PROJECT_ID=$(gcloud config get-value project)
export REGION=us-central1 
export AGENT_NAME="hello-world-agent"
export PROJECT_NUMBER=$(gcloud projects describe $PROJECT_ID --format="value(projectNumber)")
export ORGANIZATION_ID=$(gcloud projects get-ancestors ${PROJECT_ID} --format=json 2>/dev/null | jq -r '.[] | select(.type=="organization") | .id')
export USER_PROJECT_OVERRIDE=true
export GOOGLE_BILLING_PROJECT=$PROJECT_ID
Copied!
Note: If your organization ID evaluates to empty in a standalone test workspace, the lab policies automatically revert to project-level boundaries.
In the Cloud Shell terminal, copy the project directory containing the lab infrastructure and application code from your Cloud Storage bucket with the following command:

gcloud storage cp -r gs://${PROJECT_ID}-bucket/* .
Copied!
Create the AI Platform Service Agent using the following command:

gcloud beta services identity create --service=aiplatform.googleapis.com --project=${PROJECT_ID} --quiet
Copied!
Note: If the command returns a quota or rate limit error, wait a couple of minutes and try again.
Install the Google Agents CLI (agents-cli) in your Cloud Shell terminal using the following commands:

pip install --user google-agents-cli==0.6.1
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
Copied!
Install Terraform in your Cloud Shell terminal using the following commands:

wget -O - https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com/gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg
echo "deb [arch=$(dpkg --print-architecture) signed-by=/usr/share/keyrings/hashicorp-archive-keyring.gpg] https://apt.releases.hashicorp.com $(grep -oP '(?<=UBUNTU_CODENAME=).*' /etc/os-release || lsb_release -cs) main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/hashicorp.list
sudo apt update && sudo apt install terraform
Copied!
Initialize the local Terraform provider cache in your project workspace using the following command:

terraform -chdir=terraform init
Copied!
Task 2. Explore the Terraform configuration and deploy the base infrastructure
In this task, you explore the project's Terraform layout to understand how the zero-trust architecture is organized. Then, you provision a dedicated VPC network, a private subnet, a regional Private Service Connect (PSC) network attachment, and an Internal Application Load Balancer as your Agent Gateway (gateway-egress).

Explore the Terraform configuration
Before deploying, it's recommended to explore the root Terraform configurations. This helps you understand how variables will control security enforcement in subsequent tasks.

In the Cloud Shell terminal, list the contents of the terraform directory to inspect its layout using the following command:

ls -l terraform
Copied!
View the root orchestration file terraform/main.tf to locate the target modules:

module "networking": Deploys the VPC network (governed-agent-vpc), private subnet (agent-private-subnet), and Private Service Connect network attachment (gateway-egress-na).
module "registry": Establishes IAM and workspace bindings for the Agent Registry.
module "gateway": Provisions the regional Internal Application Load Balancer as the Agent Gateway (gateway-egress).
module "model_armor": Provisions Model Armor security templates (Sensitive Data Protection SSN de-identification, prompt injection filter).
Review the following core security variables in terraform/main.tf which serve as your policy switches:

enable_iap_enforcement: Set to false initially (Task 2/Task 3). You will toggle this to true in Task 4 to switch the Agent Gateway into active blocking mode.
enable_model_armor: Set to false initially. You will toggle this to true in Task 6 to activate inline content filtering.
Deploy the base infrastructure
Now you're ready to deploy the infrastructure.

In the Cloud Shell terminal, deploy the foundational infrastructure using Terraform with initial security enforcement toggles disabled (false) using the following commands:

cd ~/terraform

terraform apply -auto-approve -lock-timeout=600s \
  -var=project_id=${PROJECT_ID} \
  -var=project_number=${PROJECT_NUMBER} \
  -var=region=${REGION} \
  -var=organization_id=${ORGANIZATION_ID} \
  -var=enable_iap_enforcement=false \
  -var=agent_gateway_name=gateway-egress \
  -var=artifact_registry_repo=agent-repo \
  -var=vpc_network_name=governed-agent-vpc \
  -var=subnet_name=agent-private-subnet \
  -var=network_attachment_name=gateway-egress-na \
  -var=enable_model_armor=false \
  -var=model_armor_request_template_id=agw-request-template \
  -var=model_armor_response_template_id=agw-response-template \
  -var=model_armor_inspect_template_id=agw-ssn-inspect-template \
  -var=model_armor_deidentify_template_id=agw-ssn-redaction-template \
  -var=enable_vertex_ai_integration=true \
  -var=vertex_ai_inspect_only=false
Copied!
Note: If Terraform reports a bucket name conflict for Cloud Build staging (e.g., Bucket already exists), import the existing bucket into the Terraform state and retry using the following command:
terraform import google_storage_bucket.cloudbuild ${PROJECT_ID}_cloudbuild
Copied!
Verify that the PSC network attachment gateway-egress-na is successfully created and configured to automatically accept connections:

gcloud compute network-attachments describe gateway-egress-na \
  --region=${REGION} \
  --project=${PROJECT_ID} \
  --format="value(connectionPreference)"
Copied!
The command must output ACCEPT_AUTOMATIC.

